{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":600.9000244140625,"text":"Sometimes I feel like it's been 100 years, other times I feel like it's been five. I think the thing that's really made a material difference when I look at the ability to write software today versus what I was doing 10 years ago, was 10 years ago, it took so much time, effort, and money, and people's intellectual capital to produce a piece of software, it was almost like the ship had sailed. By the time you designed it and started writing code, it was out to sea and you were going to ride that baby for as long as you could until it got to the other side of the ocean. What I've become very comfortable with today is, for the most part, a lot of internal applications can be just thrown away. It's build them, have them work for a purpose and a point in time, but understand that they're going to disappear at some point in time, now, much more quickly than ever before, and then just think about what comes next. I think one of the challenges with sitting in my seat or any good engineer seat, should be constantly questioning, should I rewrite this? Does it cost me less to rewrite it than it costs me to keep versioning the current version forward? That is a question that, a decade ago, never would have came up in anyone's head. If you went to say, I want to rewrite an internal application, your CFO would have looked at you like you were insane, and I think now it's a completely relevant thing that all of us should be doing, because net new applications might produce way more business value at the end of the day. It's just something I encourage folks to do. It sounds like you guys are doing quite a bit at Norb. Exactly. I think it's just that concept of what used to be expensive that's now cheap. If you don't update quickly on that, then it's incredible. You said it feels like 27 or five years, or 100 years, or five years. If you over-index too hard on something that was in June, and now it's October, it feels like, in some ways, there's been 10 years of technological advancement. Great. We only have a minute or so left. Is there any closing remarks you have? The one thing I'd leave the audience on, if you want good takeaways from this, we talked about a lot of very interesting things. What makes me excited to go to work is, it's cool to get back to engineering. One of the things that I value is, I was a hardcore, low-level engineer for 10 years at IBM, and wrote, this was way back in the day, of using Notepad and VI to write instruction-level code for risk machines. It was awesome. It's nerdy as all heck. No one does this stuff anymore, but it was fun for me. I still enjoy those days. As you get more senior in your career, the barrier to actually writing code on a daily basis disappears, or it becomes more hard to get through, dealing with a stream, processing stuff off a stream. It's just all these things are like, oh, my God, I can't even get started, because the scaffolding is so hard. With AI, it's been reinvigorating to get back and actually build stuff, because it's like, I never lost my knowledge on how to architect things. I see this with a lot of engineers out there. They understand, because they've been in the market 10, 20, 30 years, they understand the complexity of system design, which hasn't changed, and now they can actually express that knowledge with these new cool agents. I think it's a super exciting time to be starting a company, working at a company, and building software, because there's a whole new world that we can create out there. That's what excites me. Yeah. Well, cool. Thank you so much. It's been an honor. Yep. Thank you guys for having us here today. I appreciate it. Thank you. Thank you.  Hi, everyone. My name is Vinay Ganesh. I'm excited to be here today. I'm the CEO and co-founder of Kepler.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":3.2799999713897705,"text":" Sometimes I feel like it's been 100 years, other times I f